154:\title[2-distance coloring of planar graphs]{A $2$-distance $(2\D+7)$-coloring of planar graphs}
162:\keywords{Coloring, 2-distance coloring, girth, planar graph.}
170:A vertex coloring of a graph $G$ is called a $2$-distance coloring if any two vertices at a distance at most $2$ from each other receive different colors.  
171:Recently, Bousquet et al. (Discrete Mathematics, 346(4), 113288, 2023)   proved that $2\D+7$ colors are sufficient for the $2$-distance coloring of planar graphs with maximum degree $\D\geq 9$. In this paper, we strengthen their result by removing the maximum degree constraint and show that all planar graphs admit a 2-distance $(2\D+7)$-coloring.
179:\section{Introduction}
184:Among the numerous problems and concepts associated with graphs, coloring stands out as a fundamental and extensively studied topic.
185:A vertex coloring of a graph assigns colors to its vertices so that adjacent vertices receive distinct colors. One particularly interesting variant of vertex coloring is 2-distance coloring, where any two vertices at a distance of $2$ or less have distinct colors. Its motivation arises from the necessity in some real-life problems to assign different colors not only to adjacent vertices but also to those in close proximity \cite{krumke,cormic}.
186:The concept of 2-distance coloring was first introduced in \cite{kramer-1, kramer-2}, and it has received considerable attention due to the fact that certain problems, such as the Total Coloring Conjecture (see \cite{daniel} for details), can be formulated as a 2-distance coloring of specific graphs.
187:A comprehensive survey on 2-distance coloring and its related coloring concepts has been presented by Cranston \cite{daniel}. 
190:The smallest number of colors for which graph $G$ admits a 2-distance coloring is known as the 2-distance chromatic number $\chi_2(G)$ of $G$. 
194:For every planar graph $G$ with maximum degree $\D$, $\chi_2(G) \leq 7$ if $\Delta=3$, \linebreak $\chi_2(G) \leq \Delta+5$ if $4\leq \Delta\leq 7$, and $\chi_2(G) \leq  \floor[\big]{\frac{3\Delta}{2}}+1$ if $\Delta\geq 8$.
198:Wegner's Conjecture is one of the most popular problems in graph coloring, and it has remained open for many years, except for the case when $\D=3$, which was solved by Thomassen \cite{thomassen} (independently by Hartke et al. \cite{hartke}). The conjecture is also known to be asymptotically true for large values of $\D$, as shown in \cite{amini,havet}.  
199:For general planar graphs,  Van den Heuvel and McGuinness \cite{van-den} showed that $\chi_2(G) \leq 2\Delta + 25$, while the bound $\chi_2(G) \leq \ceil[\big]{ \frac{5\D}{3}}+78$ was proved by Molloy and Salavatipour \cite{molloy}.
203:In this paper, we strengthen the result of Bousquet et al. \cite{bosquet} by removing the maximum degree constraint and show that all planar graphs admit a 2-distance $(2\D+7)$-coloring, which particularly improves the best-known bound of $2\Delta+25$ provided by  Van den Heuvel  and McGuinness \cite{van-den}.
